<h1>Questions and answers</h1>

<h2 id="what">What is MachXDynamics?</h2>
<p>MachXDynamics is an iPhone app, listed as {{ site.appName }} on the App Store. It shows shock waves form in a rocket nozzle, computed live on your iPhone. Drag the pressure ratio and watch the flow respond: the throat chokes, a shock forms inside the bell, the flow overexpands, matches, and finally leaves the exit underexpanded with shock cells in the jet.</p>

<h2 id="free">Is it free?</h2>
<p>Yes. {{ site.appName }} is free on the App Store. It has no in-app purchases and no advertising.</p>

<h2 id="iphone">Which iPhone do I need?</h2>
<p>Any iPhone with iOS 26 or later. The app is iPhone only and runs in landscape. It is tested on iPhone 17 Pro and smoothest on iPhone 15 Pro or newer. On older iPhones the flow takes a few minutes to settle.</p>

<h2 id="reality">How close to reality is the simulation?</h2>
<p>It is qualitative. The picture shows how the flow behaves and which way the trends go. It is not a source of design values. MachXDynamics solves the compressible flow equations on a coarse two-dimensional grid and without friction, so it can run live on a phone. The nozzle is a planar slot nozzle, not a round bell. The badge in every view says which simplifications apply.</p>

<h2 id="badge">What does the badge mean?</h2>
<p>Every view carries a short label, for example <span class="badge">2D · inviscid · no chemistry · qualitative</span>. It states what the model includes and what it leaves out: a two-dimensional flow, no friction, no chemical reactions.</p>

<h2 id="design">Can I use it to design a nozzle?</h2>
<p>No. MachXDynamics is good for seeing mechanisms and bad for design numbers. It is not a design tool. For design numbers, use a full CFD package.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-see">What can I explore in the app?</h2>
<p>A small sea-level bell and a large vacuum bell. Sweep the pressure ratio from 1 to 10,000 and watch the flow go from subsonic to choked, overexpanded, matched and underexpanded. There are Mach number, pressure and schlieren views. The ƒ(x) button opens the physics panel: the live exit Mach number, pressure ratio and flow regime, plus one sentence on what is happening and why.</p>

<h2 id="data">Does the app collect any data?</h2>
<p>No. Everything is computed on your iPhone. The app has no account, no analytics and no tracking, and it makes no network connections of its own. Details are in the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.</p>

<h2 id="who">Who makes MachXDynamics?</h2>
<p>One independent developer, from the makers of <a href="{{ site.flatsixUrl }}">FlatSixDynamics</a>. MachXDynamics is not affiliated with any space agency or launch company. The provider is named in the <a href="/impressum/">Impressum</a>. Questions go to <a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}">{{ site.contactEmail }}</a>.</p>

